Email from City of Berkeley Auditor's Office =E2=80=8A =E2=80=8A Jenny Wong=
, City Auditor Promoting transparency and accountability in Berkeley govern=
ment. =E2=80=8A Audit News from the City Auditor June 2026 Quick Links City=
 Auditor's Web Page Audits and Issued Reports Berkeley City Council Agendas=
       Join Our Mailing List Looking Ahead to FY 2027 It is June! Besides h=
aving more daylight to enjoy all Berkeley has to offer, June also marks the=
 end of the City=E2=80=99s fiscal year. Before the end of every fiscal year=
, my office releases our annual plan for what we will audit in the upcoming=
 year. See below for the list of important audits we will work on in fiscal=
 year 2027. We also just released our first ever annual Whistleblower Progr=
am report detailing the work of the program in its first calendar year, 202=
5. June also means budget season in the City of Berkeley. The City faces a =
nearly $30 million per year structural deficit in the General Fund. For mor=
e context on the City=E2=80=99s budget and broader financial condition, ple=
ase read our recent financial condition audit. Business License Taxes, Meas=
ure FF, Public Health, and Rent Board Audits Planned for FY 2027  Given Ber=
keley=E2=80=99s financial challenges, we plan to focus our work in the next=
 year on ensuring City dollars are spent responsibly and aligned with Berke=
ley values. Our FY 2027 Audit Plan includes beginning new audits of public =
health and Measure FF (streets and sidewalks). We will also complete our on=
going audits of the Rent Board and business license taxes.    We also plan =
to continue our Whistleblower Program, which just released its first annual=
 report (see below). To contribute to the City=E2=80=99s efforts to address=
 the structural budget deficit, the Auditor=E2=80=99s Office reduced our ba=
seline budget. As a result, we will pause the expansion of the Whistleblowe=
r Program to the public and have limited resources for staff training. Whil=
e these reductions will impact the office, we are committed to fulfilling o=
ur charter-mandated responsibilities. Our capacity to initiate and complete=
 projects this fiscal year will depend on available resources.    Finally, =
we will continue to track the implementation of audit recommendations throu=
gh our dashboard, and work with city departments to report on the status of=
 these recommendations through council reports.   For additional informatio=
n, you can read the full FY 2027 Audit Plan on our website.  First Annual W=
histleblower Program Report Release for 2025  Our Whistleblower Program rel=
eased its first annual report since the program launched. The Whistleblower=
 Program provides a confidential mechanism for city employees or officials =
to report concerns related to fraud, waste, and abuse of city resources. By=
 providing an independent and confidential reporting channel, the program h=
elps identify risks, improve accountability, and strengthen public trust in=
 city operations.   =EF=BB=BF Calendar year 2025 marked the first full year=
 of the Whistleblower Program. During this year, the Program received 42 ne=
w reports and closed 41 cases. Cases may be closed for several reasons, inc=
luding completion of an investigation, referral to another department or en=
tity, insufficient information to proceed, or because the allegation was ou=
tside the Program=E2=80=99s authority.  Disposition of Closed Cases (2025) =
 The Whistleblower Program may issue public reports when investigations res=
ult in substantiated findings or doing so is in the public interest. To dat=
e, we have issued a public report titled =E2=80=9CSubstantiated Whistleblow=
er Allegation: $286,000 Vehicle Purchased Without Prior Council Approval.=
=E2=80=9D   The Auditor=E2=80=99s independence ensures the integrity of the=
 Whistleblower Program, which operates as an important and trustworthy chan=
nel for employees and officials to report ethical concerns. Looking forward=
, the Whistleblower Program will continue to enhance intake and investigati=
on procedures and work closely with departments to address fraud, waste, an=
d abuse.  Check out the full report now! Budget Updates On May 19, the City=
 Council held its first public hearing on the City Manager=E2=80=99s propos=
ed budget for FY 2027 and FY 2028. The City faces a nearly $30 million per =
year structural deficit in the General Fund. Read the City Manager=E2=80=99=
s proposed plan to balance the budget. Another key part of the budget proce=
ss is the City=E2=80=99s five-year capital plan, referred to as the Capital=
 Improvement Program (CIP). Yesterday, City Council held the first public h=
earing on the proposed CIP. City Council will hold the next public hearing =
of the City budget on June 16. On June 23, City Council will hold the final=
 public hearing and consider adoption of the City budget and CIP. Visit the=
 Council agenda=E2=80=AFwebpage=E2=80=AFfor information on how to attend Ci=
ty Council meetings remotely or in-person. For more context on the City=E2=
=80=99s financial condition, please read our latest audit report. =E2=80=8A=
 =E2=80=8A Berkeley City Auditor | Website =E2=80=8A       Berkeley City Au=
ditor's Office | 2180 Milvia Street, 3rd Floor | Berkeley, CA 94704 US Unsu=
bscribe | Update Profile | Constant Contact Data Notice
